  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\n- Mild and dry weather with morning fog through the weekend\n\nOur extended stretch of pleasant fall weather continues for a few\nmore days, thanks to a building ridge of high pressure and the \nreturn of southerly low level flow. Before we get to the next few \ndays though, there is yet another potential for overnight and \nearly morning fog in several areas tonight, with perhaps a slight \nincrease in coverage as well. Light northeasterly winds are \nexpected to carry increased low level moisture into the area this \nevening, and this should boost the potential for fog as radiative \ncooling commences under clear nighttime skies. High resolution \nmodel guidance (RRFS to be specific) continues to produce a \nconvincing signal for fog locally, and when combined with the \npattern and repeated fog development over the past few days, \nanother round is expected tonight, likely with an increase in\ncoverage.\n\nFrom tomorrow through Sunday, the aforementioned upper ridge will\ncontinue to steadily build overhead, while a deep low pressure\nsystem across the Canadian Prairie drives southerly low level\nwinds. This will produce consecutive days of well above average\ntemperatures across lower Michigan (roughly 8-12 degrees above\nseasonal averages), equating to highs in the mid 60s to near 70s.\nMeanwhile, precipitation chances remain near zero throughout the\nweekend. \n\n- Cooler temperatures and modest rain chances return next week\n\nBetween Sunday night and Monday afternoon, a weak back-door cold\nfront will sink into lower Michigan as a shortwave passes well to\nour north. This will drive a steady cooling trend that will \npersist through mid-week, bringing temperatures back to near \nseasonal averages (highs in the upper 50s to low 60s / lows in the\nmod 30s to low 40s). Confidence in the temperature forecast is a\nbit lower overall on Monday, owing to ensemble member variation in\nthe timing of the cold front and trailing cold air advection. This\nuncertainty is highlighted by increasing temperature forecast \nspreads on Monday in particular, with decreasing spreads Tuesday \nonward as a low-variance high pressure pattern becomes \nestablished.\n\nMeanwhile, in spite of the presence of the cold front,\nprecipitation chances remain rather low early next week, likely\ndue to meager moisture return and weak forcing mechanisms. While \nthere remains considerable variability among ensemble members, \nlatest trends have shifted meaningful precipitation chances toward\nto mid-week period, and overall probabilities have reduced. \nStill, we do see the return of at least mentionable probabilities \nof light rain by Wednesday (20-30%), so there is at least a \nreasonable potential for some light rain toward the end of the \nforecast period.",
